The problem asks us to find the maximum number of edges that can be added to a given Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) such that the resulting graph remains a DAG. We also need to output the added edges in lexicographically smallest order.

The maximum number of edges in a DAG with $N$ vertices is $N(N-1)/2$. This is achieved by a transitive tournament, which corresponds to a topological sort where all edges go from earlier vertices to later vertices in the sort. Since the original graph is a DAG, it has at least one topological sort. Any topological sort allows us to add all missing edges consistent with it. The number of added edges is always $N(N-1)/2 - |E_{original}|$, regardless of the sort. However, different topological sorts yield different sets of added edges. We want the set that is lexicographically smallest when sorted.

To achieve the lexicographically smallest set of added edges, we should prioritize adding edges $(u, v)$ where $u < v$ over edges $(v, u)$ (where $v > u$), because $(u, v) < (v, u)$. This means we want to orient every missing edge from the smaller index to the larger index if possible. This is equivalent to finding a topological sort that respects the original constraints and also respects $u \to v$ for all $u < v$ whenever possible.

We can achieve this by iterating through all pairs $(u, v)$ with $1 \le u < v \le N$ in lexicographical order. For each pair, if there is no edge between them in the original graph, we check if adding the edge $u \to v$ would create a cycle (i.e., if there is already a path $v \leadsto u$). If no such path exists, we "add" the virtual edge $u \to v$ to our constraints (updating reachability). If a path $v \leadsto u$ exists, we are forced to have $v \to u$ in the final graph.
After processing all pairs, the relative order of every pair of vertices is fixed, defining a unique topological sort (a tournament). The set of added edges consists of all $(u, v)$ where we chose $u \to v$ (and it wasn't original) plus all $(v, u)$ where we were forced to have $v \to u$ (and it wasn't original).

To implement this efficiently for $N \le 1500$, we can use `std::bitset` to maintain the transitive closure (reachability matrix). Checking reachability is $O(1)$, and updating reachability when adding an edge $u \to v$ takes $O(N^2/64)$ or better depending on implementation. Given the constraints and typical time limits, this bitset optimization is sufficient.

Here is the C++ solution:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <bitset>

using namespace std;

// Maximum number of vertices as per constraints
const int MAXN = 1505;

// Reachability matrix: reach[i][j] = 1 if there is a path from i to j
bitset<MAXN> reach[MAXN];
// Adjacency matrix to store original edges
bool adj[MAXN][MAXN];

int main() {
    // Optimize I/O operations for speed
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(NULL);

    int N;
    if (!(cin >> N)) return 0;

    // Read the adjacency matrix
    for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {
        string s;
        cin >> s;
        for (int j = 1; j <= N; ++j) {
            if (s[j-1] == '1') {
                adj[i][j] = true;
                reach[i][j] = 1; // Direct edge implies reachability
            }
        }
        reach[i][i] = 1; // Every node reaches itself
    }

    // Compute the transitive closure of the original graph using Floyd-Warshall with bitsets
    // Complexity: O(N^3 / 64)
    for (int k = 1; k <= N; ++k) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {
            if (reach[i][k]) {
                reach[i] |= reach[k];
            }
        }
    }

    vector<pair<int, int>> added_edges;
    // Reserve memory to avoid reallocations
    added_edges.reserve(N * (N - 1) / 2);

    // Iterate over all pairs (u, v) with u < v
    // We prefer to add edge u -> v if possible to minimize lexicographical order
    for (int u = 1; u <= N; ++u) {
        for (int v = u + 1; v <= N; ++v) {
            // If there is already an edge in either direction, we don't add anything
            if (adj[u][v] || adj[v][u]) continue;

            // Check if there is a path from v to u
            if (reach[v][u]) {
                // Path v -> ... -> u exists, so we must have v before u in topological sort.
                // Thus, the edge in the completed graph will be (v, u).
                // Since (v, u) is not in original graph (checked above), we add it.
                added_edges.push_back({v, u});
            } else {
                // No path v -> u, so we can add edge u -> v.
                // This is preferred since u < v implies (u, v) < (v, u).
                added_edges.push_back({u, v});

                // Update reachability matrix to reflect the new edge u -> v.
                // If k -> ... -> u, then now k -> ... -> u -> v -> ...
                // So for all k such that reach[k][u] is true, we update reach[k] |= reach[v].
                // This propagation maintains the transitive closure.
                for (int k = 1; k <= N; ++k) {
                    if (reach[k][u]) {
                        reach[k] |= reach[v];
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Sort the added edges lexicographically as required by the problem statement
    sort(added_edges.begin(), added_edges.end());

    // Output the result
    cout << added_edges.size() << "\n";
    for (const auto& edge : added_edges) {
        cout << edge.first << " " << edge.second << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
